## PROJECT OVERVIEW

You are working on Sakaloka-Universe, a Rust-first enterprise
platform. The codebase is a Cargo workspace with strict compile-time
quality enforcement ("Iron Curtain"). Every suggestion you make must
pass the Iron Curtain or it will be rejected by CI.

The system is composed of named "planets":
- 🌍 Earth   — Loco.rs API server         (port 3000 → external 53000)
- 🪐 Jupiter — SurrealDB graph/doc DB     (port 8000 → external 58000)
- 🪐 Saturn  — Eclipse Zenoh message bus  (port 7447 → external 57447)
- 🔵 Uranus  — Qdrant vector search       (port 6333 → external 56333)
- 🔮 Neptune — Burn AI inference engine   (no HTTP, Zenoh only)
- 🌸 Venus   — React + Tauri 2.0 frontend (only external user surface)

All IAM logic lives exclusively in `libs/secure`. No planet
implements its own auth logic.

---

## WORKSPACE STRUCTURE

```
sakaloka-universe/
├── Cargo.toml          # workspace root — do NOT add [dependencies] here
├── apps/
│   ├── api/            # 🌍 Earth — Loco.rs API
│   └── web/            # 🌸 Venus — React + Vite + Tauri 2.0
├── libs/
│   ├── core/           # shared domain types, traits, constants
│   ├── data/           # SurrealDB + Qdrant + Zenoh clients
│   ├── secure/         # ALL IAM: Argon2, JWT, RBAC, tokens, keys
│   └── ai/             # Burn model loading + embed()
└── docs/               # MdBook + ADRs in /docs/adr/
```

When adding a new file, always place it in the correct crate.
Never add business logic to `apps/` — it belongs in `libs/`.

---

## THE IRON CURTAIN — NON-NEGOTIABLE RULES

These rules are enforced by the Rust compiler. Violating them
breaks the build. Never suggest code that bypasses them.

### Every `lib.rs` and `main.rs` MUST begin with:

```rust
#![deny(clippy::all)]
#![deny(clippy::unwrap_used)]
#![deny(clippy::expect_used)]
#![deny(missing_docs)]
#![forbid(unsafe_code)]
```

### The No-Panic Policy — absolute rules:

- NEVER use `.unwrap()` — use `?` or `match` instead
- NEVER use `.expect()` — propagate errors with `?`
- NEVER use `panic!()`, `unreachable!()`, or `todo!()` in non-test code
- NEVER use `unsafe { }` blocks — zero exceptions
- ALL `Result` and `Option` must be explicitly handled
- Use `thiserror` for custom error types in `libs/`
- Use `anyhow` only in binary entry points (`apps/api/src/main.rs`)

### Error handling pattern — always prefer this:

```rust
// ✅ CORRECT
pub fn parse_email(raw: &str) -> Result<EmailAddress, SecureError> {
    EmailAddress::new(raw).map_err(SecureError::InvalidEmail)
}

// ❌ WRONG — never do this
pub fn parse_email(raw: &str) -> EmailAddress {
    EmailAddress::new(raw).unwrap()
}
```

---

## DOCUMENTATION REQUIREMENTS (Luna Protocol)

Every public item in `libs/` MUST have a doc comment. This is
enforced by `#![deny(missing_docs)]`. If you write a new public
function, struct, enum, or trait without a doc comment, the build
will fail.

### Required doc comment format:

```rust
/// One-line summary of what this does.
///
/// Extended description if the logic is non-trivial.
/// Explain why, not just what.
///
/// # Errors
///
/// Returns [`ErrorType::Variant`] when <condition>.
///
/// # Examples
///
/// ```rust
/// let result = my_function("input")?;
/// assert_eq!(result, expected);
/// ```
pub fn my_function(input: &str) -> Result<Output, MyError> {
    // ...
}
```

Rules:
- Every `/// # Examples` block must be a valid, compilable snippet
- `cargo test` runs all doc-tests — broken examples break CI
- If an example needs an external service, gate it with `#[cfg(test)]`
- Private functions do not require doc comments but benefit from them

---

## IAM — IDENTITY & ACCESS MANAGEMENT

All authentication and authorization flows through `libs/secure`.
Never implement token logic, password hashing, or permission checks
anywhere else.

### Two token types — both HS256, signed with `SAKALOKA_JWT_SECRET`:

**User JWT** (issued by Earth, held by Venus):
- TTL: 15 minutes
- Claims: `sub`, `iss`, `aud`, `exp`, `iat`, `jti`, `role`, `scopes`, `session_id`
- `aud` is always `["sakaloka:earth"]`

**Service JWT** (planet-to-planet, managed by `ServiceTokenStore`):
- TTL: 5 minutes, auto-refreshed 60s before expiry
- Claims: `sub`, `iss`, `aud`, `exp`, `iat`, `jti`, `service_scopes`, `planet`
- `aud` MUST match the target planet — e.g. `["sakaloka:jupiter"]`
- A Jupiter token is REJECTED by Saturn even with a valid signature

### Key IAM rules:

- `libs/secure` is the ONLY place that calls `jsonwebtoken::encode/decode`
- `libs/secure` is the ONLY place that calls `argon2::hash_encoded/verify`
- Earth is the ONLY planet with user-facing auth endpoints
- Venus holds ONLY User JWTs — never service tokens
- Access token: stored in React state (memory only — NEVER localStorage)
- Refresh token: stored in Tauri OS keychain via `tauri-plugin-store`
- Neptune has NO HTTP port — it communicates exclusively via Zenoh

### RBAC scopes — enforce at Earth routes using `RequireScope`:

```rust
// ✅ CORRECT — protect routes at the framework level
router.route("/entity", post(create).layer(RequireScope::new(Scope::EntityWrite)));

// ❌ WRONG — never do scope checks inside the handler body
async fn create(claims: UserClaims, ...) {
    if claims.role != Role::Admin { return Err(...) } // too late, wrong layer
}
```

Scope reference:
- `entity:read`    — admin, editor, viewer, service
- `entity:write`   — admin, editor, service
- `entity:delete`  — admin only
- `search:read`    — admin, editor, viewer, service
- `user:manage`    — admin only
- `db:read/write`  — admin, service
- `db:admin`       — admin only
- `zenoh:publish/subscribe` — admin, service

---

## NEWTYPE PATTERN — INPUT VALIDATION

Never pass raw `String` or `&str` into business logic or database
queries. All external inputs must be wrapped in validated newtypes
defined in `libs/secure::newtypes` or `libs/core`.

```rust
// ✅ CORRECT
pub struct EmailAddress(String);

impl EmailAddress {
    /// Parses and validates an email address string.
    ///
    /// # Errors
    /// Returns [`SecureError::InvalidEmail`] if the string is not a
    /// valid email format.
    pub fn new(raw: &str) -> Result<Self, SecureError> {
        validate_email_format(raw)?;
        Ok(Self(raw.to_lowercase()))
    }
}

// ❌ WRONG — raw string reaches DB query
async fn create_user(email: String, db: &Surreal<Client>) { ... }

// ✅ CORRECT — validated type reaches DB query
async fn create_user(email: EmailAddress, db: &Surreal<Client>) { ... }
```

Existing newtypes (do not re-create these):
`UserId`, `TokenId`, `SessionId`, `Planet`, `ServiceName`,
`EmailAddress`, `Username`, `Password`, `PlanetName`

---

## SURREALDB — JUPITER

- All schema is defined in `.surql` files — never create tables ad hoc
- Tables use `SCHEMAFULL` — any undefined field causes a DB error
- Always authenticate using a service JWT via `DEFINE TOKEN` — never
  use the root SurrealDB password in application code
- The `jti_blocklist` table is checked on EVERY authenticated request
- Migrations must be idempotent — safe to run multiple times

```rust
// ✅ CORRECT — use the authenticated client from libs/data
let db = connect_authenticated(&config, &token_store).await?;
let user: Option<User> = db.select(("user", id)).await?;

// ❌ WRONG — never embed raw SurrealDB credentials
let db = Surreal::new::<Ws>("ws://localhost:8000").await?;
db.signin(Root { username: "root", password: "root" }).await?;
```

---

## ZENOH — SATURN

Topic naming convention — always follow this pattern:
```
sakaloka/{source_planet}/{entity}/{event_type}

Examples:
  sakaloka/earth/user/created
  sakaloka/jupiter/record/updated
  sakaloka/neptune/embedding/ready
```

Planet publish/subscribe ACL (enforced at connection time):
- Earth    → publishes: `sakaloka/earth/**`   | subscribes: `sakaloka/jupiter/**`, `sakaloka/uranus/**`
- Jupiter  → publishes: `sakaloka/jupiter/**` | subscribes: `sakaloka/earth/**`
- Uranus   → publishes: `sakaloka/uranus/**`  | subscribes: `sakaloka/jupiter/**`
- Neptune  → publishes: `sakaloka/neptune/**` | subscribes: `sakaloka/earth/**`, `sakaloka/jupiter/**`
- Venus    → NO direct Zenoh access — receives events via Earth SSE/WebSocket only

All event handlers must be **idempotent** — the same event delivered
twice must produce the same result. Use sequence numbers in payloads
to detect duplicates.

---

## QDRANT — URANUS

Never use the master Qdrant API key in application code. Always
request a scoped key from `ApiKeyVault`:

```rust
// ✅ CORRECT
let client = qdrant_client(&config, &key_vault, QdrantScope::ReadWrite)?;

// ❌ WRONG
let client = QdrantClient::from_url("http://localhost:6333")
    .with_api_key(std::env::var("QDRANT_MASTER_KEY").unwrap()) // two violations
    .build()?;
```

Scopes:
- `QdrantScope::ReadWrite` — Earth (API searches + Neptune upserts via Earth)
- `QdrantScope::WriteOnly` — Neptune (embedding upserts only, cannot search)
- `QdrantScope::ReadOnly`  — any internal read-only consumer

---

## BURN AI — NEPTUNE

- Neptune has NO HTTP server — never add one
- Neptune communicates ONLY via Zenoh (subscribes + publishes)
- The `embed()` function signature must return `Result<Vec<f32>, AiError>`
- Model is loaded from a local file path — never download at runtime
- Embedding pipeline: Jupiter record saved → Zenoh event → Neptune embeds
  → Zenoh event → Uranus upserts vector

```rust
/// Generates a fixed-dimension embedding vector for the given text.
///
/// # Errors
/// Returns [`AiError::ModelNotLoaded`] if the model file is missing.
/// Returns [`AiError::InferenceFailed`] if the forward pass fails.
pub fn embed(text: &str, model: &EmbeddingModel) -> Result<Vec<f32>, AiError> {
    // ...
}
```

